(A high intensity vs. casual speed walking intervention to reverse frailty among older) Tj T*
(adults: a cluster randomized controlled trial) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Interventions to reduce frailty and promote healthy aging are critical to improving) Tj T*
(quality of life and reducing disability in older adults. Walking is the most common form) Tj T*
(of exercise among older adults; however, the optimal walking intensity for prefrail and) Tj T*
(frail adults remains unclear. We conducted a cluster-randomized, assessor- and) Tj T*
(participant-blinded trial to determine whether high-intensity walking \(HIW\) improves) Tj T*
(frailty more than casual-speed walking \(CSW\). Fourteen retirement communities were) Tj T*
(randomized to either HIW or CSW using a modified minimal sufficient balance) Tj T*
(randomization scheme. We recruited 165 prefrail and frail adults aged ?60 years, of whom) Tj T*
(105 were included in the analytic cohort. Participants were able to ambulate at least 10) Tj T*
(feet with moderate assistance or less. The HIW group targeted 70% of heart rate maximum) Tj T*
(\(HRmax\), while the CSW group walked at self-selected speeds below 60% HRmax. Supervised) Tj T*
(walking sessions were 45 minutes, three times per week, for 16 weeks. The primary) Tj T*
(outcome was improvement by at least one frailty category on the SHARE-FI \(Survey of) Tj T*
(Health Among Retired Europeans ñ Frailty Index\). Secondary outcomes included the Timed) Tj T*
(Up and Go, Short Physical Performance Battery, Berg Balance Scale, and 6-Minute Walk) Tj T*
(Test \(6MWT\), measured at baseline and 16 weeks. Participants had a mean \(SD\) age of 79.2) Tj T*
(\(8.0\) years; 79% were female, and 75% were non-Hispanic White. Improvement in frailty) Tj T*
(category occurred in 32 of 46 \(69.6%\) HIW participants versus 26 of 56 \(46.4%\) CSW) Tj T*
(participants \(OR = 2.91, 95% CI 1.19ñ7.13, p?=?0.02\). HIW participants showed greater) Tj T*
(gains in gait speed \(mean difference?=?0.08 m/s, 95% CI 0.03ñ0.13, p?=?0.036\) and 6MWT) Tj T*
(distance \(mean difference?=?48.99 m, 95% CI 21.1ñ76.9, p?=?0.0088\). Mean HRmax was 71%) Tj T*
(in HIW vs. 59% in CSW \(p?<?0.001\). Median completed sessions were similar \(HIW?=?35;) Tj T*
(CSW?=?36; p?=?0.07\). This cluster-randomized trial demonstrates that high-intensity) Tj T*
(walking can reverse frailty more effectively than casual-speed walking due to increased) Tj T*
(physiologic stimuli during more intensive activity. This study is registered at) Tj T*
(ClinicalTrials.gov ID NCT03654807 \(https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT03709251\)) Tj T*
(Registered 10/12/2018.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Introduction) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Frailty is a geriatric syndrome in which the phenotype criteria are weight loss,) Tj T*
(fatigue, weakness, slow walking speed, and low physical activity. Physically frail) Tj T*
(individuals have three of the five criteria, while pre-frail individuals have one or two) Tj T*
([1]. Frailty is associated with a wide array of adverse health outcomes including higher) Tj T*
(health expenditures, increased risk for hospitalization, and all-cause mortality [1].) Tj T*
(With an increasingly aging global population, reducing and reversing frailty is a) Tj T*
(critical societal priority.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Physical activity \(PA\) has multi-system health benefits for older adults and is the most) Tj T*
(recommended frailty management intervention [2]. To date, there is strong evidence that) Tj T*
(exercise interventions can prevent or delay frailty progression [3]. Yet, there remains) Tj T*
(no clear evidence that existing frailty can be reversed through exercise interventions) Tj T*
(and there are no clear guidelines regarding the most effective exercise parameters to) Tj T*
(achieve this aim [3].) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Walking is the most preferred PA mode for older adults [4] and increasing daily step) Tj T*
(count may reduce the presence of frailty. Observational research finds a dose-response) Tj T*
(relationship with higher daily step counts associated with lower frailty prevalence [5].) Tj T*
(This relationship persists even among those with very low step counts \(less than 3,000) Tj T*
(per day\) suggesting any increase in step counts may reduce frailty presence. Step counts) Tj T*
(can be increased by walking for longer durations or walking faster for the same) Tj T*
(duration. Observed as the 6th vital sign, gait speed is a key frailty indicator, as slow) Tj T*
(gait speed is one of the most frequently observed and prevalent criteria of frailty [6].) Tj T*
(Since gait speed predicts mortality, falls, loss of independence, and cognitive decline) Tj T*
