BT /F1 10 Tf 40 752 Td 12 TL
([7], and is an important clinical metric of overall health [8], interventions which) Tj T*
(improve capacity to ambulate faster and over longer distances \(e.g., a greater volume of) Tj T*
(activity\) may be particularly therapeutic for those with frailty.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(While walking interventions improve functional capacity, their effectiveness in) Tj T*
(modifying the frailty phenotype remains unclear. Walking should influence at least two) Tj T*
(frailty criteria: walking speed and physical activity. In the LIFE-P study [9], older) Tj T*
(adults who walked 150 minutes each week at a low to moderate intensity had a 0.43 out of) Tj T*
(5 decrease in the number of frailty criteria present after 6 months of intervention.) Tj T*
(Interestingly, when physical activity was omitted from the frailty calculation, the) Tj T*
(intervention did not impact frailty, demonstrating that low-moderate intensity walking) Tj T*
(had null effects on the frailty syndrome while predominantly influencing physical) Tj T*
(activity. Thus, improvements in frailty after exercise training may be driven by changes) Tj T*
(in specific frailty criteria \(e.g., physical activity\) and whether these changes are) Tj T*
(potent enough to reverse the frailty syndrome remains unclear.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The primary aim of this study was to test the effect of walking intensity on frailty) Tj T*
(category at the participant level by manipulating walking speed. Those in the casual) Tj T*
(speed walking arm walked at a casual, relaxed speed targeted to be less than 60% of) Tj T*
(estimated maximum heart rate \(HRmax\) with an RPE below 13 or a moderate intensity level.) Tj T*
(Those in the high intensity walking arm walked fast with a goal for greater than 70%) Tj T*
(HRmax or greater than 15 RPE or a vigorous intensity level. The rationale for these) Tj T*
(intensity levels was to test two distinct intensity levels: moderate vs. vigorous [10].) Tj T*
(As such, our study design did not include a no-treatment control group as we were) Tj T*
(comparing active exercise strategies to determine which was more effective in reducing) Tj T*
(frailty. We defined improvement as a binary indicator of reduction by at least one) Tj T*
(frailty category on the SHARE-FI from baseline to follow-up. We hypothesized that the) Tj T*
(higher intensity intervention arm would have a greater percentage of participants with) Tj T*
(frailty reduction and improvement compared to the casual speed walking arm. The) Tj T*
(secondary aim was to test the effect of walking intensity on a battery of physical) Tj T*
(performance measures and patient-reported outcomes: SHARE-FI continuous score, mobility) Tj T*
(\(gait speed, 6 Minute Walk Test, Timed Up and Go\), physical function \(Short Physical) Tj T*
(Performance Battery, PROMIS-Global Health\), balance \(Berg Balance Scale, Falls Efficacy) Tj T*
(Scale\), and physical activity \(PA\) volume.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Methods) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Study design) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We conducted a double-masked, cluster-randomized controlled intervention trial in) Tj T*
(independent living retirement communities to investigate the effects of walking) Tj T*
(intensity on frailty category \(Aim 1\), and frailty continuous score, mobility, physical) Tj T*
(functioning, balance, and PA assessed during a one-week ambulatory monitoring period) Tj T*
(before and after the 16-week intervention \(Aim 2\). We used a cluster randomization) Tj T*
(approach to prevent contamination and keep participants blinded to group assignment by) Tj T*
(avoiding both potential and enrolled participants from observing treatment sessions of) Tj T*
(different intensities given that intervention sessions were held on-site within the) Tj T*
(retirement community in public hallways. The Northwestern University institutional) Tj T*
(review board approved the study protocol. All participants provided written informed) Tj T*
(consent after the retirement community was randomized, but prior to any intervention,) Tj T*
(and data was collected and stored through Northwesternís REDCap. The Consort diagram is) Tj T*
(shown in Fig 1 and the CONSORT checklist can be seen in S1 Checklist with the trial) Tj T*
(protocol found in S2 Protocol. The study is registered at: ClinicalTrials.gov ID) Tj T*
(NCT03654807 https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT03709251.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Study Participants) Tj T*
() Tj T*
