(provided standardized feedback to participants in both groups following a standardized) Tj T*
(script \(S3 Table in S3 File\). The only difference in intervention sessions between) Tj T*
(groups was intensity of the session which was achieved via manipulating walking speed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Primary outcome) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The primary outcome was a binary indicator of improvement by at least one frailty) Tj T*
(category defined by the SHARE-FI from baseline to 4 months. A binary indicator was used) Tj T*
(to distinguish individuals who experienced clinically meaningful improvement from those) Tj T*
(with stable or worsening frailty status. The SHARE-FI is based on a modified Fried) Tj T*
(frailty phenotypic, assesses frailty criteria through a freely accessible web-based) Tj T*
(calculator, [16] and evaluates frailty based on a measure of grip strength using a) Tj T*
(handgrip dynamometer and four self-reported items: fatigue, loss of appetite and/or) Tj T*
(eating less than usual, difficulties climbing stairs and/or walking 100 meters, and) Tj T*
(frequency of physical activity which makes it feasible and efficient for frailty) Tj T*
(administration in community settings. Both a continuous and categoric score \(e.g.,) Tj T*
(frail, pre-frail, or non-frail\) are provided. The SHARE-FI is intended for community-) Tj T*
(dwelling adults aged 50 and over and has been shown to be a valid and reliable measure) Tj T*
(of frailty [16]. For females, SHARE-FI scores?<?0.315 are considered non-frail, scores) Tj T*
(from 0.316 to 2.130 are pre-frail, and frail scores are values?>?2.131. For males,) Tj T*
(scores?<?1.211 indicate non-frail, scores from 1.212 to 3.005 are pre-frail, and frail) Tj T*
(scores are values?>?3.006.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Secondary outcomes) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Secondary outcomes included change in the SHARE-FI continuous score and a battery of) Tj T*
(physical performance and patient-reported outcome measures as outlined below.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Mobility.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Gait speed was measured over 10 meters at self-selected \(ìwalk at your normal) Tj T*
(comfortable paceî\) and fastest-possible speeds \(ìas fast as you safely canî\) with two) Tj T*
(trials averaged for analysis. The 6-Minute Walk Test was conducted in a retirement) Tj T*
(community hallways using standardized instructions and recording total meters walked.) Tj T*
(The Timed Up and Go \(TUG\) was performed using a standardized chair height at the) Tj T*
(participantís self-selected usual speed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Physical function.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We assessed physical functioning using the Short Physical Performance Balance \(SPPB\).) Tj T*
(The SPPB comprises gait speed, static balance, and sit to stand \(chair rise\) movements) Tj T*
(to provide an overall assessment of functional status and physical performance [17]. We) Tj T*
(also administered the PROMIS-Global Health questionnaire; a self-reported measure of) Tj T*
(global ratings across five domains \(physical function, fatigue, pain, emotional) Tj T*
(distress, and social health\) [18].) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Balance.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To assess balance, we used the Berg Balance Scale and Falls Efficacy Scale. The Berg is) Tj T*
(a valid and reliable 14-item tool to evaluate static and dynamic balance among older) Tj T*
(adults. Scores on the Berg range from 0ñ56 with scores of 0ñ20 indicating high fall) Tj T*
(risk, scores of 21ñ40 indicating medium fall risk, and scores of 41ñ56 indicating low) Tj T*
(fall risk [19]. We administered the Falls Efficacy Scale questionnaire, a widely-used) Tj T*
(measure among older adults that evaluates the fear of falling and balance confidence. In) Tj T*
(this 10-item questionnaire with scores ranging from 0 to 100, participants rated their) Tj T*
(confidence in performing a variety of tasks using a 10-point Likert Scale. Scores of) Tj T*
(70ñ79 indicate a fear of falling while scores of 80ñ100 indicate a fall risk [20].) Tj T*
