(Sample size considerations) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A priori sample size calculations were based on detecting differences in the proportion) Tj T*
(of improvement in SHARE-FI category between arms. We anticipated the CSW intervention) Tj T*
(arm to exhibit small improvements in frailty category, ranging from 1%?10%. We) Tj T*
(anticipated an approximate 25%?50% improvement in the HIW arm, corresponding to a) Tj T*
(difference of 20%?45% between arms. With limited knowledge on intra-cluster correlation,) Tj T*
(power considerations allowed for varying levels of correlation within cluster. Target) Tj T*
(sample sizes of 16 participants per site with 8 sites total, for a total of 128) Tj T*
(participants, provided adequate power to detect these differences under a range of) Tj T*
(assumptions. Sample sizes were inflated to assume 20% attrition at both the site and) Tj T*
(participant level for enrolling 10 retirement communities with 20 participants each. Our) Tj T*
(final analytic sample size \(assuming 7 CSW clusters with an average cluster size of 7) Tj T*
(and 5 HIW clusters with an average cluster size of 12\) provided 90% power to detect) Tj T*
(differences in proportions of 17%?30%.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Results) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Retirement communities) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Fourteen independent living retirement communities were enrolled and randomized \(n?=?8) Tj T*
(in HIW and n?=?6 in CSW\). One community did not allow participant recruitment after) Tj T*
(randomization and was withdrawn. Estimates of the minority resident population ranged) Tj T*
(from 0% to 100% \(median?=?11.7%; IQR 1%, 63%\) and cognitive impairment ranged from 0% to) Tj T*
(30% \(median?=?6.3%; IQR 1.3%, 10%\) based on self-reported data from retirement community) Tj T*
(administrators. Communities ranged in size from 100 to 600 residents, were in urban and) Tj T*
(suburban areas throughout the Chicagoland, Illinois, USA metropolitan area, and varied) Tj T*
(in rent rates ranging from $1,300 to over $8,000 USD per month rent.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Baseline characteristics of participants) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We screened 215 participants for eligibility from 13 randomized retirement communities;) Tj T*
(165 \(77%\) were enrolled and 105 \(49%\) completed the intervention and were included in) Tj T*
(the analytic cohort: n?=?47 in HIW and n?=?58 in CSW \(mean [SD] age, 79.2 [8.05]; age) Tj T*
(range 62ñ96 years; 83 [79%] were female; 79 [75%] were non-Hispanic White and 65% used) Tj T*
(an assistive device when ambulating \(Table 1\). Of note, n?=?34 in HIW and n?=?26 in CSW) Tj T*
(were not included in the analytic cohort due to withdrawal, lost-to-follow-up, or death) Tj T*
(\(Fig 1\). At baseline, 41% of participants were frail and 59% were pre-frail. Groups were) Tj T*
(well matched across demographic characteristics \(Table 1\). Table 4 in S3 File shows the) Tj T*
(participant demographics and baseline outcome measures between included and excluded) Tj T*
(participants. Participants who were excluded due to not completing follow-up measures) Tj T*
(were slightly older and had worse physical function.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Primary outcomes) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the HIW group, 69.6% of participants had an improvement in SHARE-FI category compared) Tj T*
(to 46.4% of CSW participants \(Table 2 and Fig 2A\). We found significantly increased odds) Tj T*
(of improving at least one SHARE-FI frailty category in the HIW group \(OR=2.91 [reference) Tj T*
(group?=?CSW], 95% CI 1.19, 7.13, p?=?0.02, N?=?102\). The per-protocol analysis yielded) Tj T*
(consistent results, identifying a significant increase in odds of improving by at least) Tj T*
(one frailty category \(OR = 3.41 [reference group?=?CSW], 95% CI 1.32, 8.84, p?=?0.01\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Sensitivity analyses under various missing data assumptions are shown in S3 Table 5 and) Tj T*
(Table 6 in S3 File. The most conservative sensitivity analyses assuming all participants) Tj T*
(lost to follow-up resulted in an attenuated odds ratio for improving at least one SHARE-) Tj T*
(FI frailty category \(OR=1.51 [reference group?=?CSW], 95% CI 0.72, 3.13, p?=?0.27\).) Tj T*
