(P-value?=?0.036\) \(Fig 2C\), as well as 6-Minute Walk Test \(mean difference?=?48.99m, 95%) Tj T*
(CI?=?21.1, 76.87, Hochberg adjusted P-value?=?0.0088\) \(Fig 2D\). There was a significant) Tj T*
(between-group difference in physical activity as measured by activPAL mean steps per day) Tj T*
(\(mean difference?=?985 steps/day, 95% CI?=?427, 1543, Hochberg adjusted P-value?=?0.01\).) Tj T*
(The per-protocol analysis yielded consistent results identifying significant differences) Tj T*
(in usual gait speed \(Mean difference?=?0.08 meters/sec \(0.02, 0.14\), unadjusted) Tj T*
(p-value?=?0.01\) and 6 Minute Walk Test \(Mean difference?=?50.93 meters \(16.10, 85.75,) Tj T*
(unadjusted p-value?=?0.005\) favoring the high-intensity intervention. While not) Tj T*
(significant between groups, the change in fast gait speed and SPPB between baseline and) Tj T*
(follow up in both treatment groups was greater than the MCID of 0.05 m/s for gait speed) Tj T*
([23] and 1.0 for the SPPB [24]. There were no significant differences in any other) Tj T*
(secondary outcomes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Treatment fidelity) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Participants in the HIW group averaged 71% of HRmax during walking sessions while those) Tj T*
(in the CSW group averaged 59% of HRmax \(p?<?0.001\) \(Fig 2E\). Median number of sessions) Tj T*
(completed in the HIW group was 35 and was 36 in the CSW group \(Fig 2F\). Eighty-five) Tj T*
(participants \(81%\) completed at least 4 out of 7 tasks for at least 29 of the 36) Tj T*
