(Safety and adherence) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A common criticism of high intensity training for older adults with frailty is safety) Tj T*
(and injury risk. [35]. Across 3,354 sessions in this trial, 0.3% of sessions were) Tj T*
(associated with an adverse event providing strong evidence that HIW is safe for those) Tj T*
(with frailty. Another concern of HIW is that participants will be less adherent to more) Tj T*
(intensive activity [36]. Adherence between treatment arms was similar: HIW at 73% and) Tj T*
(CSW at 75% refuting concerns that older adults cannot sustain higher-intensity activity.) Tj T*
(Importantly, participants self-selected their pace and this approach produced clear) Tj T*
(heart rate differences between groups which achieved targeted intensities. Others have) Tj T*
(found that self-selecting intensity level during exercise rather than having it imposed) Tj T*
(may foster greater tolerance to higher intensity levels [37]. Our study provides) Tj T*
(evidence that older adults can self-select a speed that can achieve more intensive) Tj T*
(exercise. As many exercise guidelines instruct people to achieve ìmoderate to vigorousî) Tj T*
(levels of activity, it may be that a lay-friendly instruction such as ìwalk as fast as) Tj T*
(you canî is more easily understood by older adults and able to be acted upon.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Contributions to research) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Our study has four major strengths. First, the intervention was conducted in retirement) Tj T*
(communities demonstrating the real-world practicality of this approach. Second, this) Tj T*
(intervention was supervised with scripted, standardized prompts, reducing the likelihood) Tj T*
(of a research assistant-effect on outcomes. Third, we compared two active interventions,) Tj T*
(both of which were beneficial, avoiding the now scientifically and ethically) Tj T*
(questionable no-exercise comparator. Finally, our rigorous design addressed common) Tj T*
(limitations in frailty research by 1\) using frailty as both an inclusion criterion and) Tj T*
(an intervention outcome via a validated frailty tool, 2\) masking both assessors and) Tj T*
(participants to knowledge of treatment allocation, 3\) leveraging the same scripts to) Tj T*
(motivate participants across groups to minimize the research assistant effect on) Tj T*
(participants, and 4\) directly comparing two exercise intensities.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Limitations) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Although enrollment mirrored the demographics of senior living residents, 75% of our) Tj T*
(participants were white and 89% were female. Thus, generalizability of our findings to) Tj T*
(diverse populations needs further investigation. Although this study focused on an) Tj T*
(exercise-based intervention, frailty is widely recognized as a multidimensional) Tj T*
(geriatric syndrome influenced by multiple interacting factors. While exercise remains) Tj T*
(one of the most consistently supported interventions for improving physical function and) Tj T*
(frailty-related outcomes, multidomain approaches incorporating nutrition, cognitive) Tj T*
(engagement, and comprehensive geriatric management may provide additional benefit.) Tj T*
(Future research should explore integrated interventions that address these broader) Tj T*
(contributors to frailty. One factor that potentially drives the strong adherence to the) Tj T*
(protocol in this study could be the supervised nature of the program. Further work) Tj T*
(should explore if the protocol could be delivered and achieve similar results in) Tj T*
(unsupervised or group settings. We acknowledge a large drop-out rate from enrollment to) Tj T*
(analysis \(36%\). Attrition is a widespread challenge in exercise programs and an) Tj T*
(estimated 50% of participants drop out of exercise programs in the first 6 months [38].) Tj T*
(We found the baseline characteristics of the full enrollment sample and the analytic) Tj T*
(sample were not substantively different. While sensitivity analyses using imputed data) Tj T*
(did not preserve the significant treatment effect, the magnitude of the effect remained) Tj T*
(clinically significant. Finally, we did not capture any longer-term outcomes from the) Tj T*
(intervention or any co-interventions \(ex. Physical therapy or structured group exercise) Tj T*
(participation\). Future work should ascertain the impact of co-interventions as well as) Tj T*
(the impact on longer-term outcomes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Conclusion) Tj T*
ET
